---
scope: scripts/buildReadme
alignment: unreviewed
description: Generates the README's tools section from the per-tool reference pages, so the README always lists every tool the server offers and CI fails when the committed copy drifts.
intent: >
  The README's tools section is written from the project's per-tool reference
  pages, never by hand, so it cannot fall behind the tools the server offers.
  Each tool appears with the one-sentence description its page
  gives and a link to its page on aidemd.dev, in the tools index's grouped
  order. Only that section is replaced; the rest of the README is never touched.
  A check in the CI test suite fails whenever the committed section
  differs from what would be generated now. Regeneration is a developer command,
  kept out of the build so a stale copy cannot hide from CI.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every tool with a reference page appears in the README's tools section exactly once, in the tools index order, with the description its page gives word for word.
    - Every tool's link opens that tool's own page on aidemd.dev.
    - Every byte of the README outside the generated section, line endings included, is identical after a run, and a second run changes nothing.
    - CI fails whenever the committed tools section differs from what the generator would write now, so a new, renamed or removed tool cannot leave the README behind.
    - A missing or doubled section marker stops the run with a clear error that names the problem.
  undesired:
    - A tool missing from the section or listed twice, or the section still listing a tool that no longer has a reference page.
    - A description paraphrased, shortened or truncated instead of taken as the tool's page gives it.
    - A link that points at a page that does not exist on aidemd.dev.
    - Edits leaking outside the generated section, such as reflowed text, changed line endings or touched headings.
    - The drift check passing only because something, such as the build, regenerated the README before it ran.
    - A missing or doubled marker handled by guessing where the section is and writing there, instead of stopping.
---
